    <changeSet id="patientview-2026-08-10-00" author="patientview-roadmap">
        <comment>
            Ensures a named, non-unique index on patient_id exists before we touch anything else in
            this table below, so that when patientview_medhist_patient_fk is dropped and recreated
            in patientview-2026-08-10-01, MySQL attaches it to this explicit index rather than
            silently auto-creating its own unnamed one.
        </comment>
        <createIndex indexName="idx_patientview_medical_history_patient" tableName="patientview_medical_history">
            <column name="patient_id"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="patientview-2026-08-10-01" author="patientview-roadmap">
        <validCheckSum>8:630aa09ce9504a3cb72fde081d3ef62d</validCheckSum>
        <validCheckSum>1:any</validCheckSum>
        <comment>
            Non-repudiation fix: patientview_medical_history was originally one mutable row per
            patient (unique patient_id), so an edit silently overwrote the previous values with
            no trace of who changed what. Drop the uniqueness constraint so saves can append a
            new row instead of updating in place, matching the append-only pattern already used
            by every other table in this module. The DAO now always inserts; "current" state is
            simply the most recent row per patient.

            This changeset's content is now effectively frozen: it went through two revisions
            after already having run successfully in at least one real deployment (first a plain
            DROP INDEX, which hit MySQL error 1553; then this drop-FK/drop-index/recreate-FK
            version). Editing an already-executed changeset's SQL doesn't change what already ran
            - Liquibase treats a changeset ID as permanently "done" once any checksum for it is
            recorded, and validCheckSum only silences the mismatch warning, it does not trigger a
            re-run. The &lt;validCheckSum&gt;1:any&lt;/validCheckSum&gt; entry above is there so this stops being a landmine for
            every future edit to this comment. The real fix, that actually re-verifies and repairs
            the schema on every startup regardless of which partial state a given database is in,
            is the new changeset below (patientview-2026-08-12-01) - treat that one as the source
            of truth for this migration, not this one.
        </comment>
        <dropForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="patientview_medical_history"
            constraintName="patientview_medhist_patient_fk"/>
        <sql>
            SET @uq_name = (
                SELECT tc.CONSTRAINT_NAME
                FROM information_schema.TABLE_CONSTRAINTS tc
                JOIN information_schema.KEY_COLUMN_USAGE kcu
                  ON kcu.CONSTRAINT_NAME = tc.CONSTRAINT_NAME
                 AND kcu.TABLE_SCHEMA = tc.TABLE_SCHEMA
                 AND kcu.TABLE_NAME = tc.TABLE_NAME
                WHERE tc.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE()
                  AND tc.TABLE_NAME = 'patientview_medical_history'
                  AND tc.CONSTRAINT_TYPE = 'UNIQUE'
                  AND kcu.COLUMN_NAME = 'patient_id'
                LIMIT 1
            );
            SET @uq_drop_sql = IF(@uq_name IS NULL, 'SELECT 1',
                CONCAT('ALTER TABLE patientview_medical_history DROP INDEX `', @uq_name, '`'));
            PREPARE uq_drop_stmt FROM @uq_drop_sql;
            EXECUTE uq_drop_stmt;
            DEALLOCATE PREPARE uq_drop_stmt;
        </sql>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint constraintName="patientview_medhist_patient_fk"
            baseTableName="patientview_medical_history" baseColumnNames="patient_id"
            referencedTableName="patient" referencedColumnNames="patient_id"/>
        <rollback>
            <!-- Not reversible in general (the original unique constraint's name is not
                 recoverable), and not needed in practice - reintroducing uniqueness here is only
                 relevant to Phase 2 development, not to a real deployment's rollback path. -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="patientview-2026-08-12-01" author="patientview-roadmap" runOnChange="true">
        <comment>
            The actual, authoritative fix for patientview_medical_history's non-repudiation
            migration - written to be fully idempotent and state-based (it checks what currently
            exists rather than assuming what an earlier changeset did or didn't manage to do), so
            it reaches the correct end state no matter which of these three situations a given
            database is in: (1) never touched by any earlier version of this migration, (2) fully
            migrated already, or (3) partially migrated by an earlier, since-corrected attempt.

            End state, verified fresh on every run: no unique constraint on
            patientview_medical_history.patient_id, an ordinary supporting index on that column
            (idx_patientview_medical_history_patient), and patientview_medhist_patient_fk present.

            runOnChange="true" so that if this logic itself ever needs a fix, editing it here is
            enough - Liquibase re-runs a runOnChange changeset whenever its checksum changes,
            unlike the frozen one above.
        </comment>
        <sql>
            SET @uq_name = (
                SELECT tc.CONSTRAINT_NAME
                FROM information_schema.TABLE_CONSTRAINTS tc
                JOIN information_schema.KEY_COLUMN_USAGE kcu
                  ON kcu.CONSTRAINT_NAME = tc.CONSTRAINT_NAME
                 AND kcu.TABLE_SCHEMA = tc.TABLE_SCHEMA
                 AND kcu.TABLE_NAME = tc.TABLE_NAME
                WHERE tc.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE()
                  AND tc.TABLE_NAME = 'patientview_medical_history'
                  AND tc.CONSTRAINT_TYPE = 'UNIQUE'
                  AND kcu.COLUMN_NAME = 'patient_id'
                LIMIT 1
            );
            SET @fk_exists = (
                SELECT COUNT(*)
                FROM information_schema.TABLE_CONSTRAINTS
                WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE()
                  AND TABLE_NAME = 'patientview_medical_history'
                  AND CONSTRAINT_NAME = 'patientview_medhist_patient_fk'
                  AND CONSTRAINT_TYPE = 'FOREIGN KEY'
            );

            -- Step 1: only touch the FK if it currently exists AND the unique constraint we need
            -- gone is still there (i.e. it's actually in the way).
            SET @fk_drop_sql = IF(@fk_exists > 0 AND @uq_name IS NOT NULL,
                'ALTER TABLE patientview_medical_history DROP FOREIGN KEY patientview_medhist_patient_fk',
                'SELECT 1');
            PREPARE fk_drop_stmt FROM @fk_drop_sql;
            EXECUTE fk_drop_stmt;
            DEALLOCATE PREPARE fk_drop_stmt;

            -- Step 2: make sure a plain supporting index exists before/regardless of dropping the
            -- unique one, so nothing ever needs the unique index to satisfy an FK again.
            SET @plain_idx_exists = (
                SELECT COUNT(*)
                FROM information_schema.STATISTICS
                WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE()
                  AND TABLE_NAME = 'patientview_medical_history'
                  AND INDEX_NAME = 'idx_patientview_medical_history_patient'
            );
            SET @create_idx_sql = IF(@plain_idx_exists = 0,
                'CREATE INDEX idx_patientview_medical_history_patient ON patientview_medical_history (patient_id)',
                'SELECT 1');
            PREPARE create_idx_stmt FROM @create_idx_sql;
            EXECUTE create_idx_stmt;
            DEALLOCATE PREPARE create_idx_stmt;

            -- Step 3: drop the unique constraint if it's still there - safe now regardless of
            -- whether step 1 just removed the FK or it was never present to begin with.
            SET @uq_drop_sql = IF(@uq_name IS NULL, 'SELECT 1',
                CONCAT('ALTER TABLE patientview_medical_history DROP INDEX `', @uq_name, '`'));
            PREPARE uq_drop_stmt FROM @uq_drop_sql;
            EXECUTE uq_drop_stmt;
            DEALLOCATE PREPARE uq_drop_stmt;

            -- Step 4: re-check fresh (don't reuse @fk_exists, which may now be stale) and add the
            -- FK back if it's missing for any reason, whether this run dropped it or it was
            -- already missing before this changeset ran at all.
            SET @fk_exists_now = (
                SELECT COUNT(*)
                FROM information_schema.TABLE_CONSTRAINTS
                WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE()
                  AND TABLE_NAME = 'patientview_medical_history'
                  AND CONSTRAINT_NAME = 'patientview_medhist_patient_fk'
                  AND CONSTRAINT_TYPE = 'FOREIGN KEY'
            );
            SET @fk_add_sql = IF(@fk_exists_now = 0,
                'ALTER TABLE patientview_medical_history ADD CONSTRAINT patientview_medhist_patient_fk FOREIGN KEY (patient_id) REFERENCES patient (patient_id)',
                'SELECT 1');
            PREPARE fk_add_stmt FROM @fk_add_sql;
            EXECUTE fk_add_stmt;
            DEALLOCATE PREPARE fk_add_stmt;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- Not reversible in general (see patientview-2026-08-10-01's rollback note) and not
                 needed in practice. -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

